        "conclusion": "explained",
        "defect_claim": null,
        "rationale": "State of this run. Asserted, each scoped to its executed basis: (1) a follower that has installed a snapshot for (L,T) but still advertises an earlier term for index L rejects the leader's next AppendEntries with PrevLogEntry=L/PrevLogTerm=T (retained CheckRun 72367650c8d0470d8197a7d2c0f97ce1, indices 5 and 6 against control 1 and 2); (2) with TrailingLogs=0 and a snapshot taken at the log end, the leader cannot repair such a follower - it re-sends the whole snapshot while the follower's applied index stays at the snapshot index (single-peer witness CheckRun 7772ad1b5b154d498a27a79806bb0ea7, indices 3 and 1; sealed-partition witness on a produced five-node history CheckRun f66fa4e4e0514f7e95b7788032144a30, indices 1, 2 and 9); (3) the load-bearing difference for the non-zero-TrailingLogs cases is how much of its log the leader retains below the snapshot index at snapshot time (control CheckRun beca431464bf44ab9c3354145c21899f, indices 9 and 19). Narrowed and partly refuted along the way: an earlier negative reading of the five-node history was an artifact of an incomplete partition and its Fact was revised in place; the generalisation that any non-zero TrailingLogs merely delays the loop was refuted (CheckRun 0b99df9ed72e4cd2b653c5741e4eec3d); and the further generalisation that the compacted-boundary condition is sufficient was refuted by the backlog case in the same receipt family (CheckRun 62f089bd843641099558ff8d8831ab29, indices 35 and 43). For a human reviewer the defensible statement is therefore: with TrailingLogs=0 and a snapshot taken at the log end, a rejoining follower can be unrepairable and the leader loops on whole-snapshot transfers; with non-zero TrailingLogs the executed histories converge, and the compaction condition does not by itself predict the loop. Still open: (a) the mechanism by which the backlog-case peer was repaired - its store ended at 61..62 while the leader's was 63..64 at the same applied index 64 and it had applied 61 commands, which is consistent with the peer snapshotting on its own under the shared small SnapshotThreshold and is not isolated; (b) whether the default TrailingLogs (10240) with a matching backlog reproduces the loop, which was never executed; (c) the older registered gaps that this run answered only in prose, because their investigation ids were not resolvable for formal answers. One record inconsistency should be read with this entry: 7465f7b9e6cd44e39e8cbf7e2c45c955-interpretation-0 still reads as refuting the loop for the five-node history, but its Fact was revised (revision 2) and the lineage entry 059c0fbb2e8849188bb09529ad9118a8-interpretation-0 explains that the refutation applied only to the incomplete-partition harness.",
        "answers": {},
